## Bring up a clean run

1. Pin the lead and each specialist by full SHA. Use Git LFS to hydrate large files into independent read-only source copies and verify the reference manifest. Preserve the archived experimental flags and record the exact retained screen flags. Keep all temporary tooling, browser profiles, model copies and captures separate from protected checkouts.
2. Use the official Blender archive and release checksum. Install the pinned Image2Blender Codex plugin into a disposable profile if reconstruction is needed. Allow its single official Blender Lab MCP entry to reach the background add-on on loopback. Read the strict spec and source assets before invoking modeling passes. Shut down the connection when the session ends.
3. In a temporary tools directory use the provided package.json and package-lock.json with `npm ci --ignore-scripts --no-audit --no-fund`. The published files include integrity hashes. To use Spector, check out its exact repository revision, use the separately included upstream MCP lockfile with the same npm options, build its TypeScript MCP, and supply the pinned local Spector browser bundle. The tested Spector worker used the installed Chromium 156 via an isolated headless launcher pointing to that executable. Its own Playwright lock lists Chromium 158. The recorded WebGL functionality was exercised on 156.
4. Start the local Vite builds on loopback. Supply each precise retained query from `queries.json`. Use a fresh browser profile with no account cookies and block external page requests. Keep the Chrome CDP endpoint local and disable DevTools usage statistics and CrUX queries. Microsoft and Chrome can share the diagnostic browser. Spector launches a separate isolated browser only for its frame probe.
5. For the Three inspector, bind its proxy to loopback, turn off its overlay, wait for Rainbow's QA interface, and publish the actual scene, renderer and camera from `__rainbow.qa3d()` to the inspector's explicit handles. Inject the pinned inspector in the temporary tab after those handles exist. Automatic scene discovery selected a postprocessing scene during the first test. Successful object discovery returned all five named Screen 07 paddles.
6. Read the pinned project's capture and gameplay scripts before reusing the evidence harness. The included `evidence/run/motion.cjs` is the run-specific replay fixture. It expects localhost ports 5604–5608 and 5664 for the independent Screen 04 ring copy, reads the included flags and commits, and writes to a sibling captures directory. The project’s original gameplay assertion body is included at the path it reads. Set the Node module search path to your verified Playwright installation and supply Chromium at the executable path in the fixture. Run `node motion.cjs 04 05 06 07 08 04r`. In a new capture directory run `node fresh-opening.cjs 04 05 06 07 08 04r` for independent opening repeats. The compare helper in the proposed skill accepts the original encoded references and native captures.
7. Record retained and candidate footage at the same course positions. Verify each MP4 with ffprobe and compare encoded frame count with the route log. Review the full ride and the frames near large changes. Save the separate approved, before and candidate images, pose logs and regression output. Verify any explicitly approved Git push against its remote ref.

This run used SwiftShader software WebGL. Its diagnostic draw and geometry counts help identify content issues. Device frame rate, thermal load and touch performance require a run on Android hardware. Persist the recipes, source SHAs, .blend/GLB archives and evidence wherever the owner authorizes. Temporary installs and running backup processes depend on a live workspace.

